## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account security, least privilege, encryption, and auditability in a realistic data-lake setup.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles with MFA
- S3 Access Points for per-tenant isolation
- KMS key policies with rotating grants
- CloudTrail + AWS Config for compliance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Trust policy (JSON)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::DATA_SCI_ACC:role/Tenant42DataScientist"},
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
    "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:MultiRoleSession": true}}
  }]
}
```

```javascript
// Permissions policy (JSON)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
    "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake-tenant42/*", "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake-tenant42"]
  },{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": ["kms:Decrypt"],
    "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:REGION:ACCOUNT:key/KEYID"
  }]
}
```

```javascript
// KMS key policy (JSON)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::DATA_SCI_ACC:root"},
    "Action": ["kms:Decrypt"],
    "Resource": "*"
  }]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests proficiency designing fine-grained, scalable access controls for multi-tenant data lakes using ABAC, tags, and policy syntax.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tags and aws:PrincipalTag
- Resource policies with aws:RequestTag and stringEquals conditions
- S3 bucket prefixes per tenant and least privilege
- Testing cross-tenant access and tag revocation

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (IAM role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy (alternative strict guard)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:ExistingObjectTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tags and ensure no stale access remains?
- How would you integrate with CI to validate policies in a sandbox before prod?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical use of tenancy isolation with minimal privileges and serverless flows, a common real-world pattern for SaaS apps.

## Key Concepts

- API Gateway input validation and headers
- Lambda least-privilege IAM roles
- S3 presigned URLs for controlled uploads
- Tenant isolation via per-tenant buckets/prefixes
- Basic negative testing and expiry behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const tenantId = event.headers?.TenantId;
  if (!tenantId) throw new Error('Missing TenantId');
  const bucket = `tenant-${tenantId}`;
  const keyPrefix = `uploads/${Date.now()}_${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2)}/`;
  const params = {
    Bucket: bucket,
    Key: keyPrefix,
    Expires: 300,
  };
  const url = s3.getSignedUrl('putObject', params);
  return { url };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce TLS and HTTP security at API Gateway?
- How to audit and rotate tenant credentials or bucket policies?
- What changes would you make for multi-region availability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-region, multi-account DR with strict IAM boundaries and automated failover.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-region replication (S3 CRR, DynamoDB Global Tables)
- Route 53 failover with health checks
- Per-tenant IAM roles with least privilege and explicit denies
- IaC automation and DR testing

## Code Example

```terraform
# Minimal Terraform snippet for Route53 failover
resource "aws_route53_health_check" "region_a" {
  fqdn = "api.example.com"
  type = "HTTPS"
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "primary" {
  name = "api.example.com"
  type = "A"
  zone_id = var.zone_id
  set_identifier = "RegionA"
  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "primary"
    health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.region_a.id
  }
  alias {
    name = aws_lb.region_a.dns_name
    zone_id = aws_lb.region_a.zone_id
  }
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "secondary" {
  name = "api.example.com"
  type = "A"
  zone_id = var.zone_id
  set_identifier = "RegionB"
  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "secondary"
    health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.region_b.id
  }
  alias {
    name = aws_lb.region_b.dns_name
    zone_id = aws_lb.region_b.zone_id
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify data consistency after failover?
- What monitoring alerts would you add?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft strict per-tenant isolation across multiple AWS accounts, combining STS-based role assumption, granular S3 bucket policies, KMS key rotation, and an auditable governance workflow. It also exercises incident response via quarantine and DynamoDB auditing.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles and trust policies
- S3 bucket policies with prefix-scoped access
- AWS KMS CMK rotation and key policy implications
- Lambda-based policy enforcement and DynamoDB auditing
- Canary testing for tenancy violations and quarantine workflows

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::INGEST_ACCT_ID:role/TenantAppRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::telemetry-bucket/tenants/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenantId}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption": "aws:kms"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      ""Action": ["kms:Encrypt","kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:REGION:ACCOUNT_ID:key/CMK_ID",
